<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Sestos</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Sestos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek city on the European shore of the Hellespont, key to the grain route, whose capture by Athens in 478 BCE closes Herodotus's Histories.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sestos was founded by Aeolian settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a>, at the narrows of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> opposite <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a>. Its harbour and position made it the main crossing point from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province">Asia</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sestos lay near the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes's</a> bridges in 480 BCE. In 479 to 478 BCE the Athenians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanthippus-father-of-pericles">Xanthippus</a> besieged and took it, the episode that ends <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus's</a> Histories. It became a key Athenian base for protecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> grain route and paid tribute in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>. Greek legend made it the home of Hero, beloved of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hero-and-leander">Leander</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Athenian defeat at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aegospotami">Aegospotami</a> in 405 BCE, Sestos passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a>, then back to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and later to Macedonian and Hellenistic rulers. It came under Roman control by 146 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sestos.jpg" alt="Sestos" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: Jyglesias3 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thracian_Chersonese.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sestos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aeolic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sestus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-373&amp;focus=sestos" aria-label="Sestos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5329 739 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sestos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5649 995L6140 1251M5649 995L5591 1068M5649 995L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5649 995h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5649 995h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-373&amp;focus=sestos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

